==English==

===Etymology===
As if {{term|smack|smacked|hit}} in the {{term|gob||mouth (Irish / Scottish gaelic)}}.

Attested since 1980s, from Northern English dialect, particularly Liverpool, popularized via television.<ref>[http://www.worldwidewords.org/qa/qa-gob1.htm World Wide Words: Gobsmacked]</ref>

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{chiefly|British|slang}} [[flabbergasted|Flabbergasted]], [[astounded]], [[speechless]], [[overawed]].
#* '''1989''', Glenn Frankel, "Salman Rushdie's Life on the Run," ''Los Angeles Times'', 7 Aug.,
#*: We were as appalled and stunned and confused and '''gobsmacked''' (punched on the mouth) as anyone else.
#* '''2008''', Caroline Mallan, "Linwood Barclay novel wins a plug on key UK book list," ''Toronto Star'', 16 Jun., p. A2,
#*: "I guess the word would be '''gobsmacked'''," Barclay said, of his reaction.  "I am stunned."

